From fdf9329ecd51a5b82162e9d5c5ce7412f1530e43 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HelaFaye <dilsha21@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 14:19:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 19/26] esp32s3: Wi-Fi frames, to and from an emulated access
 point

With -nic user,model=esp32_wifi, the S3's Wi-Fi MAC moves frames: its transmit
DMA hands each frame to an access point emulated in QEMU, which answers probes,
authentication and association for its networks, beacons on their channels,
and bridges data frames to QEMU's network (user-mode: DHCP, the host at
10.0.2.2). Without it, Wi-Fi starts and finds nothing, as before.

The MAC and access point are Martin Johnson's ESP32 emulation
(github.com/a159x36/qemu), whose access point is Clemens Kolbitsch's QEMU WLAN
emulation (MIT). Changed here, for ESP-IDF 5.4 on the S3:

- the receive registers its driver follows: the last descriptor filled (0x90,
  its high bits at 0xc64), the next one (0x8c), and a reload once descriptors
  are appended after the DMA ran out (0x84); without them the driver followed
  a null descriptor and panicked;
- the access point bridges by the frames' addresses (ToDS address 2 and 3 to
  the Ethernet source and destination, and back), so it works with any
  station MAC, not one fixed address;
- with no eFuse file, the chip's factory MAC is the NIC's (unique, or mac=),
  where it was 00:00:00:00:00:00;
- RSSI in dBm; ssid= names the open network; reset clears the receive state;
- the channel comes from the RF PLL's frequency in the analog block, now kept
  to 0x6000E1FF.

Test: esp32s3-wifi-test (a beacon received; the receive registers; a reload;
a probe answered; nothing heard off channel).
